WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.482
The first CTE remains at admission grain and adds previous discharge.

00:00:04.482 --> 00:00:10.327
The second CTE keeps only admissions with a previous encounter and calculates the gap.

00:00:10.327 --> 00:00:15.852
The final query joins patient names and applies the thirty-day classification.

00:00:15.852 --> 00:00:19.087
Run it and read the query stage by stage.

00:00:19.087 --> 00:00:24.292
Notice that the patient join is delayed until the final result because it is descriptive rather than needed

00:00:24.292 --> 00:00:31.752
for sequence logic. When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:31.752 --> 00:00:37.897
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:37.897 --> 00:00:40.307
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:40.307 --> 00:00:46.462
In a real setting such as an operations team comparing each event with the previous and next event,

00:00:46.462 --> 00:00:52.607
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:52.607 --> 00:00:54.722
before discussing syntax.

00:00:54.722 --> 00:01:00.872
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
